remasters have become an accepted part  of video games in recent years older  games are given a fresh coat of paint  and sometimes even gameplay tweaks and  then released on modern systems  this year alone has seen some pretty big  remasters including Bioshock call of  duty 4 modern warfare and the  elderscrolls v Skyrim but let me ask you  a question  can remasters change too much because  the reality is that these remasters  aren't the same game you played all  those years ago they feature different  textures employed modern-day tech that  simply wasn't possible back then these  aren't upscale transfers of a VHS to  blu-ray these are Star Wars special  editions so what happens when these  updated versions alter too much of the  original art style and gameplay tweaks  that fundamentally make the game  different the Bioshock remaster has been  panned by fans for numerous reasons  including limited graphic options change  to art missing audio and somehow being  even more buggy than the original PC  port but I want to focus on the change  in art because bioshock's art style is  probably its most recognizable aspect  and core to its identity the changes  made in the remaster are pretty subtle  and the average person will probably not  even notice them things like added  seaweed in the bathysphere ride this  statue of Andrew Ryan has been remodeled  that red carpet has been removed and for  some reason the floor tiles in this room  have been rotated the lighting is  different in places and the textures are  less wet which is to say they are no  longer glossy and that feeling of being  in a damp City at the bottom of the  ocean is somewhat diminished and the  truth is a lot of people will not even  notice or care about these small changes  but for some these details are important  because you're playing off of people's  nostalgia and odd changes like the  removal of this carpet for seemingly no  reason other than to just make it  different feels pointless it's like when  George Lucas added this rock in front of  r2d2 it doesn't ruin the movie or  anything but just why but maybe carpet  changes and not wet textures feels too  nitpicky for you how about more obvious  and dramatic changes like the iOS remake  of Final Fantasy 6 for reference here is  the original Final Fantasy 6 for the  Super Nintendo  for the mobile version they stretched  the image then they took the backgrounds  and appear to have traced them over the  character sprites are then completely  replaced everything is given this weird  blurry smudgy effect and then the text  box has been replaced with modern UI  font and a detailed character portrait  the final effect is a combination of  disparate styles that lacks the  cohesiveness of that original pixel art  rather than rebuild the whole game with  a new artistic vision we get this  Frankenstein Photoshop which mistreats  the original art and the artist who made  it  but you won't just see the difference  individuals sometimes remasters add  gameplay changes as well which makes  sense controls and certain mechanics can  quickly become outdated in the  fast-moving world of video games so  wanting to freshen them up for modern  audiences can be a good idea Nintendo's  been doing this with all of their  remasters for the 3d Zelda games  addressing complaints with the originals  and fixing them where they can stuff  like adding the fast sail in Wind Waker  HD to cut down a long sailing time or  changing the song of double time in  Majora's Mask 3ds which lets you skip to  any hour of the day instead of Vanaras  half day these design changes help  remove a lot of the tedium from these  games and all of this is fantastic but  we also need to be careful sometimes in  what we change a video game and its  mechanics are tied to each other and  it's possible to unintentionally alter  the experience too much  such is the case with Doom threes  remaster BFG edition doom 3 has a  controversial flashlight mechanic  because you could not use it and hold a  weapon at the same time this urged a lot  of players leading to the creation of  the infamous duct tape mod so when the  BFG edition came around it made the bold  move of making this mod official  removing the original flashlight  mechanic from the game entirely  it wasn't even available as a menu  option and this is a problem because  even if you didn't like it  doom 3 was designed around that original  flashlight every room was a potential  for a scare and the comfort you would  get from being able to see what the  flashlight came at the cost of being  able to shoot taking that away removes a  large part of the tension and suspense  of doom 3 creates it makes the game  worse  and while doom 3 hasn't aged terribly  well the flashlight is still a unique  idea that you don't see replicated in  other games it's a concept wholly  original to doom 3 and they removed it  but that's ok because we can still load  up and enjoy original doom 3 well that  almost wasn't the case back when doom 3  BFG edition came out original doom 3 and  its expansion were removed from the  steam store no official reason was ever  said for why this happened but if you  want to put on your Cinna cat it could  have been because you could just buy the  original game cheaper and then recreate  all of bf G's improvements with mods the  original game came back to the store a  month later allowing consumers the  choice in which version to buy but this  isn't always the case if you want to  play the horror classic style Hill 2 &amp; 3  which you should your only choice on  modern systems is to buy the HD  remasters which have been heavily  criticized as Rush port jobs that iOS  version of Final Fantasy 6 that's the  version that got ported to Steam and  this is becoming a real issue with games  on digital stores publishers seem  interested in only having one version of  their game on a storefront so if  multiple versions exist they tend to  pick the most recent version a perfect  example of this is the original Resident  Evil Resident Evil One has numerous  ports and updates over the years and  even a full-on excellent remake but if  you still want to play the ps1 classic  it's available digitally on the  PlayStation Store except that's actually  not the original release of the game  it's the Resident Evil  rector's cut DualShock version which was  released over a year after the original  game and features added content such as  arranged mode which swaps around all the  puzzle items it's certainly the most  feature-rich version which is probably  why it's the one on the store but our  EDC DSV also features a completely  different soundtrack from the original  one that is worse but because this is  the only version that got picked to be  preserved digitally that original  soundtrack will eventually be lost to  time and this will be replaced with this  more so than any other media games  change especially in today's era of  updates and patches it's impossible for  someone starting destiny today to be  able to play and experience the original  vanilla release from 2014 it's okay and  even important for games to change and  for devs to be allowed to make those  changes but it's also important to  respect the original versions of these  games and give players choices in the  versions they can play when the original  system shock was finally available  digitally developer night dive Studios  put out the Enhanced Edition which  updated the graphics and improved the  controls a welcome addition because  those original controls were garbage but  it also comes with the classic version  of the game for those that want to  experience it and then there are games  that allow you to switch between  original and updated controls audio and  graphics in the games themselves games  like the Halo anniversaries and the  LucasArts adventure game remasters you  press a button and suddenly you're  swapping between ten years of video game  progress apart from it just being a  really cool gimmick it acts as a  historical showcase and that's what  these remasters should be celebrations  of these games and the time in history  that comes with them which also means  being willing to show the flaws rather  than sweep them under the rug  I want remasters to be the Blade Runner  box set that comes with all five cuts  even if I will never wash the attribute  version because preserving and  respecting games is important and we  could be doing a lot better job at it
 

